# Copy to .env and fill in from Supabase → Project Settings → API Keys.
#
# Both values are safe in client code and end up readable inside the published JavaScript
# bundle. That is by design: they identify the *project*, not a *person*. What a caller may
# actually read or write is decided by their login token against the row-level security rules
# in supabase/schema.sql.
#
# Use the PUBLISHABLE key (`sb_publishable_...`). On older projects the same key is labelled
# "anon public" and looks like `eyJ...` — same thing, same place, either works.
#
# NEVER use the "secret" key (`sb_secret_...`, formerly "service_role"). It bypasses every
# security rule, and anything shipped to a browser is public.
#
# The variable name below stays VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY regardless of which label the dashboard
# shows — renaming it would mean touching the code, the workflow and the GitHub secrets for no
# behavioural gain.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT-REF.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=sb_publishable_...
